{
  "id": "Compound::DB00777 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Propiomazine is likely to interact with Chrm3 through a mechanism involving the G-protein coupled receptor system. The evidence suggests that Propiomazine binds to Chrm5, which is a receptor that participates in multiple receptor activities, including G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity, muscarinic acetylcholine receptor activity, and G-protein coupled neurotransmitter receptor activity. These activities are all associated with Chrm3, indicating a potential pathway where Propiomazine influences Chrm3 via Chrm5. The overlapping participation of Chrm5 in different receptor activities implies that Propiomazine may modulate Chrm3 through a shared receptor mechanism, possibly by affecting the signaling pathways downstream of these receptors. The consistent binding to Chrm5 and the involvement of Chrm3 in receptor activities suggest a plausible, though not yet fully elucidated, relationship between Propiomazine and Chrm3.",
